<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <RootNamespace>FlowX.Postgres.Tests</RootNamespace>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk" Version="18.11.0" />
    <PackageReference Include="xunit.v3" Version="4.0.2" />
    <PackageReference Include="xunit.runner.visualstudio" Version="4.0.1" />
    <PackageReference Include="Shouldly" Version="4.3.0" />
    <PackageReference Include="coverlet.collector" Version="10.1.0" />
    <!--
      The container implementation, not just its abstractions: the registration test builds a
      provider and asks which service types AddFlowXPostgres actually produced. Asserting on
      the ServiceDescriptor list instead would assert on what was written down rather than on
      what a host resolves. Pinned, like every other dependency here.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection" Version="10.0.0" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="../../plugins/FlowX.Postgres/FlowX.Postgres.csproj" />

    <!--
      The host, so that "a Postgres-backed node recovers an abandoned instance" is asserted
      as a host recovering an instance rather than as a query returning a row. The adapter
      itself still depends on nothing but FlowX.Abstractions — this reference belongs to the
      test project, not to the package, and RuntimeDoesNotReferenceAnyPlugin is untouched
      because the arrow points from a test at the runtime.

      It is worth the reference specifically because every piece of this was already correct
      in isolation. The journal passed JournalConformance, the lease store passed
      LeaseStoreConformance, FlowRecoveryScan passed its own tests against an in-memory
      double, and a Postgres host still swept for nothing at all, silently, because the
      interface that joins them is resolved as an optional service.
    -->
    <ProjectReference Include="../../src/FlowX.Hosting/FlowX.Hosting.csproj" />
    <!--
      The conformance suite is referenced, not copied. ADR-0009 makes it the semantic
      contract for every store, and docs/17-Plugin-System.md §5 says a third party claims
      conformance by deriving from it — so this project derives from it exactly as a third
      party would, from a different assembly, with the suite unmodified. Copying an
      assertion into this project would prove something about the copy.

      Nothing in tests/FlowX.Conformance.Tests changed to make this work, which is the
      result worth having: the suite was derivable from outside without an edit.
    -->
    <ProjectReference Include="../FlowX.Conformance.Tests/FlowX.Conformance.Tests.csproj" />

    <!--
      The compiler, as an ANALYZER. This is the only place besides samples/ecommerce where
      the generator runs inside a real build, and the only place anywhere where it runs
      beside System.Text.Json's own generator over a DURABLE flow that emits.

      That combination is the point. The emitter's own tests assert the text of a generated
      DescribeStep; nothing there proves the text binds against a context another generator
      produced in the same compilation, and nothing there could — one generator does not see
      another's output, so the question is only answerable by compiling. Without this
      reference EmitReachesTheBrokerTests does not compile, which is the loud version of the
      same fact.
    -->
    <ProjectReference Include="../../src/FlowX.Compiler/FlowX.Compiler.csproj"
                      OutputItemType="Analyzer"
                      ReferenceOutputAssembly="false" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
